    "preview": "Acknowledge, then hold.",
    "html": "<p>Hi {{first_name}},</p>\n<p>Guilt is the most common pressure people like us face. \u201cAfter everything I\u2019ve done for you.\u201d \u201cI\u2019ll just do it myself, like always.\u201d \u201cI thought I could count on you.\u201d</p>\n<p>Each one takes a request you could easily decline and attaches a moral weight to it. Saying no stops being about your schedule and starts being about your character.</p>\n<p>The reply has a simple shape: <strong>acknowledge the feeling, then hold the decision.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\u201cYou\u2019ve done a lot for me, and I\u2019m grateful. I\u2019m still not able to do this.\u201d</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\u201cI know it feels like a lot. I can\u2019t help this weekend.\u201d</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You don\u2019t argue about whether they really do everything. You don\u2019t defend your record. You just don\u2019t let the guilt change the answer.</p>\n<p>One test helps: when you feel guilty, ask whether you actually broke a promise or caused harm. If not, the guilt isn\u2019t telling you about right and wrong. It\u2019s just pressure.</p>\n<p>Chapter 11 covers six kinds of guilt trip, each with its own reply.</p>\n<p>Nate</p>",
    "text": "Hi {{first_name}},\n\nGuilt is the most common pressure people like us face. \"After everything I've done for you.\" \"I'll just do it myself, like always.\" \"I thought I could count on you.\"\n\nEach one takes a request you could easily decline and attaches a moral weight to it. Saying no stops being about your schedule and starts being about your character.\n\nThe reply has a simple shape: acknowledge the feeling, then hold the decision.\n\n\"You've done a lot for me, and I'm grateful. I'm still not able to do this.\"\n\n\"I know it feels like a lot. I can't help this weekend.\"\n\nYou don't argue about whether they really do everything. You don't defend your record. You just don't let the guilt change the answer.\n\nOne test helps: when you feel guilty, ask whether you actually broke a promise or caused harm. If not, the guilt isn't telling you about right and wrong. It's just pressure.\n\nChapter 11 covers six kinds of guilt trip, each with its own reply.\n\nNate"
